• React: It is a popular JavaScript tool for building fast, interactive website user interfaces out of small, reusable pieces.
• Components: They are the independent, reusable building blocks of a React website, like a button, a search bar, or a navigation menu.
• Props (Properties): They are pieces of information passed down from a parent component to a child component to customize how it looks or acts.
• State: It is a component's personal memory that stores changing data, like whether a menu is open or what a user has typed into a box.
• Event handler: It is a function that waits for a user action—like clicking a button or pressing a key—and runs specific code in response.
• Asynchronous function: It is a function that starts a slow task (like fetching data from the internet) and pauses its own steps without freezing the rest of the website.
• Clipboard API: It is a built-in browser feature that lets your website safely copy text to or paste text from the user's device clipboard.
• Error handling: It is writing backup safety code (like try/catch blocks) to catch mistakes gracefully so your app shows a helpful message instead of crashing.
• GitHub pull request (PR): It is a formal request asking a project's owners to review your code changes and merge them into the main project.
• Open-source contribution: It is improving a publicly shared software project by fixing bugs, adding features, or updating documentation for the community.
